---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';

const page = {
  title: "Bootable FAQ — Safety, verification, and supported media",
  description: "Straight answers about Bootable's disk safeguards, verification scope, Windows 11 media, image formats, administrator access, cancellation, and alpha limits.",
  ogTitle: "Bootable FAQ",
  ogDescription: "Safety, verification, supported media, administrator access, and current limits.",
  ogType: "website",
  current: "faq",
  footerLinks: [ { label: "Features", href: "/features" }, { label: "Guides", href: "/guides" }, { label: "Download", href: "/download" }, { label: "Safety", href: "https://github.com/debpalash/bootable/blob/main/docs/safety.md" }, { label: "Security", href: "https://github.com/debpalash/bootable/blob/main/SECURITY.md" }, { label: "Source", href: "https://github.com/debpalash/bootable" } ],
  crumbs: [{ label: "Home", href: "/" }, { label: "FAQ" }],
};
---
<Layout {...page}>
<main class="guide-main">
  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={page.crumbs} />
      <header class="guide-hero">
        <p class="eyebrow">Frequently asked questions</p>
        <h1>Short answers. Exact limits.</h1>
        <p class="lede">Disk safety, verification scope, and current limits.</p>
      </header>

      <section class="guide-index faq-list" aria-label="Questions and answers">
        <article>
          <h2>What does Bootable write?</h2>
          <p>ISO, IMG, and RAW images; XZ, gzip, Zstandard, and bzip2 archives; and extracted FAT32 Windows installer media. Sources can be local files or downloads from the built-in catalog.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h2>Can it erase an internal disk?</h2>
          <p>Bootable rejects fixed disks, the running system disk, partitions, read-only media, and write plans that do not fit the expanded image. It accepts whole USB-attached or explicitly removable disks, then rechecks the stable target identity immediately before writing. This is alpha software: always verify the physical device named in the review.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h2>Why does writing require administrator access?</h2>
          <p>Operating systems restrict raw disk access. Interactive writes elevate only a fixed, protected helper after the write plan is approved. The desktop, TUI, catalog, downloads, and image parsing stay unelevated.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h2>What does “verified” mean?</h2>
          <div>
            <ul>
              <li>A publisher checksum match proves the source bytes match the publisher's checksum file.</li>
              <li>Raw read-back proves the written range matches the expanded source stream.</li>
              <li>A Windows boot-tree audit checks UEFI files, installer payloads, split-WIM parts, and FAT32 limits.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>None of these proves that every firmware or operating-system installation will succeed.</p>
          </div>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h2>Can it create a Windows 11 USB on Linux or macOS?</h2>
          <p>Yes. Bootable creates GPT or MBR media with one FAT32 partition and splits an oversized <code>install.wim</code>. Linux needs <code>wimtools</code>; macOS needs <code>wimlib</code>. Current Windows media targets UEFI, not legacy BIOS.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h2>Which Windows setup choices are available?</h2>
          <p>Hardware-requirement bypass, offline-account path, named local account, host regional settings, privacy defaults, automatic BitLocker control, quality-of-life policies, CA 2023 boot files, SkuSiPolicy revocations, and S Mode. Each choice appears in the reviewed write plan.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h2>What happens if I cancel a write?</h2>
          <p>Bootable stops at a controlled boundary, terminates media tools it started, and marks the target incomplete. Do not boot or reuse that media. Start a full write again.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h2>Are the desktop and terminal apps equivalent?</h2>
          <p>Yes. GUI and TUI use the same labels, section order, plans, progress stages, cancellation, errors, and retry behavior. Both call the same core and protected helper.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h2>Is Bootable a drop-in replacement for Rufus or Etcher?</h2>
          <p>It covers the common raw and compressed image workflow across Linux, Windows, and macOS, and it can construct UEFI Windows installer media on all three. It is not feature-for-feature complete: persistence, multiboot, DOS media, Windows To Go, legacy-BIOS helpers, and general-purpose formatting are not implemented. Bootable is independent and is not affiliated with Rufus, balena, or Etcher.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h2>What is not implemented?</h2>
          <p>Legacy BIOS helpers for constructed Windows media, Linux persistence, Windows To Go, DOS media, and multiboot. Windows RAW backup is also unavailable. See the <a href="https://github.com/debpalash/bootable/blob/main/docs/roadmap.md">roadmap</a> before depending on a planned feature.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h2>Why does the operating system warn about Bootable?</h2>
          <p>The alpha executables are not code-signed and the macOS build is not notarized. Release archives include SHA-256 sidecars and GitHub build provenance, but those do not remove SmartScreen or Gatekeeper warnings.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h2>How should I report a failed write?</h2>
          <p>Use the <a href="https://github.com/debpalash/bootable/issues/new?template=alpha-report.yml">alpha report</a>. Include version, platform, interface, image name and version, target model and capacity, failed stage, and exact error. Remove disk serial numbers, usernames, and private paths.</p>
        </article>
      </section>
</main>
</Layout>
